:    The *actual* setup was this:

:    You have a box, a cat, and a vial of poison.  Cat and poison are in the 
: box.  If you break the vial which is inside the box and release the 
: poison, at any given time you cannot predict whether or not the cat is 
: dead, but you know that sometime between the vial breaking and the 
: removal of the dead cat, the cat dies.

Um, this is a quantum example, and I've always heard it stated as one.
The (odor-proof) box starts out containing one live cat, one
radioactive atom, and a device that will kill the cat when that atom
decays, possibly involving a vial.  Radioactive decay has a half-life:
the wave function exponentially moves from the state ``atom'' to the
state  ``decayed atom'' with that half-life.  Thus the cat moves from
the state ``alive'' to ``dead'' with that same half-life.  The idea
being that the wavefuntion has to be tested before it ``knows'' what
the choices are, and further that the cat doesn't know how to do the
test.  When you open the box some time later, there is a probability
t/\tau that the cat will still be alive, where \tau is the e-folding
lifetime of the radioactive atom.

It's a radioactive atom, with a well-defined QM behavior, that does
the Rube-Goldberg killing of the cat.  The point of the experiment is
how literally to take the wavefunction idea.  There isn't really an
answer.

